1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qd3 { Now why bring the queen out to d3, rather than the bishop? The reason is that White intends to transfer the queen to h4 after Black responds with ... d5xe4 and ...Nf6 } 4... dxe4 { Once again Black’s most logical response is to head for a Rubinstein
structure when we are guaranteed a tempo gain. } 5. Qxe4 Nf6 6. Qh4 { The queen is White’s Marie Antoinette, who doesn’t worry about the
development of any of the other pieces in White’s army. It is move six and White moved his queen for 50% of his moves, so } 6... Nbd7 { I prefer to develop rather than break with ... c7-c5 right away. } 7. Bd3 c5 { This thematic break eliminates White’s last central pawn. } 8. Nf3 cxd4 9. Qxd4 Qb6 { My natural inclination is to always beg to swap queens whenever possible. 9
... Qe7 also looks even. } { [%cal Gd4b6,Gd7b6] } *


