1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bd2 dxe4 5. Qg4 (5. Nxe4? Qxd4 6. Bd3 Bxd2+ 7. Qxd2 Qxb2! 8. Rd1 Nc6 9. Nf3 Nge7 10. Nc3 a6 $17) 5... Nf6 { Just as in the 4 a3 lines, Black is happy to give up g7 in return for the e4
pawn. } (5... Qxd4 { is Black’s main alternative, yet it feels like the move plays into
White’s hands, since we lose time after } 6. Nf3 Nh6 7. Qf4 (7. Qxe6+ Bxe6 8. Nxd4 Bd7 9. Nxe4 Bxd2+ 10. Nxd2 $10) 7... e5 8. Nxd4 exf4 9. Ndb5 Na6 10. Bxf4 $14 { I like White’s slight development lead in the ending }) 6. Qxg7 Rg8 7. Qh6 Qxd4 8. O-O-O (8. Nge2 Qe5 $7 9. O-O-O Rg6 $15) 8... Bf8! { This move wins a second pawn. } 9. Qh4 Rg4 10. Qh3 Qxf2 $15 { This is a key position in our line:
1. For now Black is up two pawns.
2. White has a development lead and is already castled.
3. For now White controls the dark squares.
The theoretical consensus is that the game is dynamically balanced, while the greedier comp slightly prefers Black’s side. } *


