1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qg4!? { Cautious players like me tend to police themselves, while people who play
this line with White don’t. This is perhaps White’s most risky fourth move
alternative, although the comp thinks it is still playable for White. With the early queen move White neglects development, while swapping a central pawn for a wing pawn. } 4... Nf6 { We are happy to swap g7 in exchange for e4, since in doing so we gain time. } 5. Qxg7 Rg8 6. Qh6 c5 { Principles: Open the game and create confrontation when leading in
development. } 7. a3 (7. e5 cxd4 8. a3 (8. exf6 { ჩემი დამატებულია } 8... dxc3 9. b3 Nd7 10. Bg5 Bf8 11. Qh4 h6 $17) 8... Bf8 $15) 7... Rg6 8. Qh4 Qa5 { Black makes use of the pin on the a-file to exploit his development lead. } (8... Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 dxe4 { reaches a position we can get via the 4 a3 lines. }) 9. Ne2 { The c3 and d4 points must be reinforced: } 9... cxd4 10. axb4?! (10. e5 { this is correct move } 10... Ne4 { This was not in the book } 11. axb4 Qxa1 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. Kd1 $7 Nc6 14. Ng3 $7 Nxe5) 10... Qxa1 11. Nb5 dxe4 12. Qf4 Nd5 $16 *


