1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. Ngf3 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 6. Bb5 (6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd3 Nf6 8. O-O Be7 9. b3 O-O 10. Bb2 a5 11. a4 Ba6) 6... Bd7 7. Nxc6 (7. Bxc6 { White would rather lose a tempo than lose his light-squared bishop } 7... bxc6 8. O-O Bd6 9. Re1 Ne7 10. e5 Bc7 11. N2b3 O-O 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bh4 Bb6 14. a4 a5 15. Qg4 Qe8! { Black stands better since White’s attack isn’t dangerous while
Black owns the bishop-pair, with ... c6-c5 to come and then ... Nf5 }) 7... bxc6 8. Bd3 Qc7 9. Qe2 Ne7 { One thing to remember is in this line the g8-knight’s most
harmonious post is g6, via e7. } 10. Nf3 Ng6 { Now ... Nf4 is in the air. } 11. e5 { White wedges his e-pawn to the fifth rank, while preventing ... Nf4. } 11... c5 { Now ... c5-c4 is the strategic threat. } 12. c4 dxc4 { Principle: Meet a (coming) wing attack with a central counter. } 13. Bxc4 Rb8 14. O-O Be7 15. b3 { The idea is to back up the e5-pawn with a fianchetto and then attack on the
kingside. } 15... Bc6 16. Bb2 O-O *


