1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. exd5 Qxd5 { Principle: Don’t bring out your queen early in the game, yet in this case the
violation is justified/mitigated by several factors:
1. This recapture with the queen avoids an isolani position by avoiding the ...exd5 lines, although it doesn’t exactly strike us as one filled with Napoleonic ambition.
2. White’s knight is already committed to d2, so there is no normal Nc3, as
we see in the Scandinavian.
3. Bc4 is possible, but so what? White’s bishop hits a pawn wall on e6. Also,
it may be vulnerable to a future ... a7-a6 and ... b7-b5, as well as ... Rc8. } 5. dxc5 (5. Ngf3 { 5 Ngf3 is White’s main move, which we look at next. }) 5... Nf6 { 1. Black’s queen is exposed to attack by white minor pieces on d5. So by
playing ... Qxc5, it allows her to slip away to c7, our intended destination, where she will be safe.
2. Black’s dark-squared bishop will probably be needed on e7, or perhaps d6. So by taking on c5, the bishop, in an odd way, actually wastes time. } 6. Ngf3 (6. b4?? Qe5+ { [%cal Ge5a1] }) (6. Nb3!? { White tries to hang on to the c5-pawn at the cost of losing time } 6... Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 Bd7 { at some point Black can play ... Bc6 and ... Nbd7, adding heat to c5 } 8. f3 Be7 9. Na5 Bc6 10. Be3 Nbd7 11. c3 Bxc5 12. Bxc5 Nxc5 13. Nxc6 bxc6 $10) 6... Qxc5 7. Bd3 Nbd7 { Here d7 is better than c6 for the knight, for the following reasons:
1. On d7, the knight reinforces the f6-knight against Bg5 and Bxf6 ideas
from White.
2. On d7 the knight doesn’t block the black queen’s access to c7.
3. On d7, the knight may later harass White’s light-squared bishop with ...
Nc5 } 8. O-O Qc7 { This is the third queen move out of eight by Black’s crazy queen. White, though, isn’t in a position to seriously exploit the development lead, despite the semi-open nature of the position. Why is this? Because Black’s formation is ultra solid and can only be torn apart by some kind of piece sacrifice, which really doesn’t exist. } 9. Qe2 Nc5 10. Bc4 Bd6 11. b3 a6 12. a4 b6 { Otherwise White plays a4-a5. } 13. Bb2 Bb7! *


