1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. Nf3!? cxd4 { If White allows us to swap our wing c-pawn for the central d4-pawn, I
believe we should do so. } 5. Bd3 { White sacrifices the d4-pawn to seize space with the e5-pawn. White then proceeds to overprotect e5, in case Black counters with ... f7-f6. } (5. Nxd4 Ne7 6. Nc3 Nbc6 7. Bb5 a6 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 9. O-O c5 10. Nf3 Ng6 11. Re1 Be7 12. g3 Bb7 13. h4 h6 14. h5 Nf8 { I love Black’s position, since he controls greater central territory, owns the bishop-pair and has strong potential on White’s weakened light squares, }) 5... Nc6 6. O-O Nge7 { 6 ... f6 is also possible, but it feels too early to me to open the centre when
White leads in development. } 7. Bf4 { Nimzowitsch-approved overprotection of e5. } 7... Ng6 (7... Qb6 { I would stay away from pawn grabbing at the cost of development } 8. Nbd2 Qxb2 9. Nb3 Qa3 10. h4 { Black is up material, but he also lags seriously in development. }) 8. Bg3 Be7 { Black logically completes development before venturing either ... f7-f6 or ...
f7-f5. } 9. a3?! O-O 10. Re1 f5 { [%cal Gf5f4] } 11. h3 Bd7 12. b4 a5! { This creates holes in White’s position. } 13. b5 Na7 14. a4 Bc5 15. Nbd2 Nc8 16. Nb3 Bb4 *


