1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. Qg4?! { To me, reason feels absent in this line. This is an old Nimzowitschian
concoction, which today is known to be dubious, although it is still dusted off occasionally by club level players as a surprise weapon, so we must be ready for it. The idea is to keep Black tied down to defence of g7 at the too high cost of violating principle by developing the white queen too early. She will be vulnerable to tempo loss and also White’s d4-pawn isn’t properly supported. } 4... Nc6 (4... cxd4 { Possible too } 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Bd3 Qc7 7. Qg3 Nge7 8. O-O Ng6 9. Re1 f6! 10. Bxg6+ hxg6 11. Qxg6+ Qf7 12. Qg3 { an exchange of queens leads to a highly favourable ending for Black, who owns the bishop-pair and dominates the centre } 12... Be7 13. Bf4 Bd7 { with the bishop-pair, greater central control and an open h-file. } 14. exf6 gxf6) 5. Nf3 (5. c3? cxd4 6. cxd4 Qb6 7. Nf3 Nh6! 8. Qf4 Nf5 { and Black wins a pawn. }) 5... Nge7 6. c3!? { To my mind this move just leads to a normal position from the Advanced
French, except that White tossed in the undesirable Qg4. } 6... cxd4 7. Bd3 (7. cxd4 { also looks miserable for White after } 7... Bd7 8. Nc3 Nb4 9. Rb1 Qb6 10. a3 Nc2+ 11. Kd1 Qb3) 7... Ng6 8. Qg3 dxc3 9. Nxc3 { We rarely spot combinations in the opening, mainly since we don’t believe
they exist this early and tend not to look. } 9... Bd7! (9... d4?! { Just because we see a combination, doesn’t automatically mean we should
play it. I’m actually not so convinced that this is a good move, even though it
picks up a second pawn, since Black falls behind in development and it also
allows White to greatly increase the level of complications. I would ignore it and just keep developing if I played Black. } 10. Ne4 Ngxe5 11. Nxe5 Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Qxe5) 10. O-O (10. h4 Qc7 11. Nb5 Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Nb4!) 10... Be7 11. Re1 O-O { and I don’t see White’s compensation. } *


