1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. dxc5?! { gives away a good central pawn for Black’s wing pawn, while weakening e5, for no discernible compensation. My feeling is that Black already stands better if you know the theory. } 4... Nc6 { An important in-between move for us to remember. I learned long ago that 4 ... Bxc5?! is inaccurate due to 5 Qg4 and here I grudgingly played 5 ... Kf8!?. I just turned White’s semi-dubious opening into perfectly acceptable one, due to my inaccuracy, } 5. Nf3 Bxc5 { Now that White’s Qg4 is cut off, this move is safe to play. } 6. Bd3 f6! { I like this move better than 6 ... Nge7 } 7. Qe2 (7. exf6?! { assists Black’s development } 7... Nxf6 8. O-O O-O 9. c4 dxc4! 10. Bxc4 Qxd1 11. Rxd1 Ng4 12. Rf1 Nd4! 13. Nbd2 b5 14. Bd3 Bb7 { and White’s position was under tremendous pressure }) 7... fxe5 (7... Qc7 { Also worthy of consideration } 8. Bf4 g5! 9. Bg3 g4 10. Nfd2 Nxe5 { I have doubts about White’s full compensation for the pawn }) 8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. Qxe5 Nf6 { [%cal Gc5f2,Gf6g4] } 10. Bb5+ Kf7 { Renewing the ... Bxf2+ threat. The loss of castling doesn’t bother Black, for
two reasons:
1. Black can castle by hand with ... Rf8 and ... Kg8.
2.The bishop check on b5 represents an equivalent loss of time for White. } { [%cal Gc5f2,Gf6g4] } (10... Bd7?? 11. Qxe6+) 11. Qe2 { A new move } (11. O-O Rf8 12. Nd2 Kg8 $17 { [%cal Gf6g4,Ge6e5] }) 11... Rf8 12. O-O Kg8 { That wasn’t so hard. Black’s position is active with an open f-file and the
stronger centre. } 13. Nd2 e5 14. h3 (14. Qxe5?? Ng4) 14... a6 15. Ba4 e4! { აქ დებიუტს დავასრულებ და დანარჩენუ უბრალოდ საინტერესოა } 16. c4 b5 (16... Qc7! { even stronger } { [%cal Gc7g3] } 17. Kh1 (17. cxd5 Qg3 18. Kh1 Qh4) 17... Ba7! 18. cxd5 Bb8!) 17. cxb5 Qe8 (17... Qc7! { again misses the idea } 18. Nb3 Bxh3! 19. Nxc5 Ng4) 18. Nb3 Bd6 19. Re1 Qg6 *


