1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Qb6 8. Nc3 (8. Be2 Nge7 9. Nc3 Nf5 10. Na4 Qa5+ 11. Nc3 (11. Bd2 Bb4)) (8. Bc2 Nb4 9. O-O (9. Ba4 Qa6 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7)) 8... Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. O-O a6 11. Qe2 Ne7 12. Kh1 (12. Rd1 Nc6 13. Bxa6 Qxe5 14. Bxb7 Qxe2 15. Nxe2 Rb8 16. Bxc6 (16. Ba6 Nb4) 16... Bxc6 17. Nd4 Bd7) 12... Nc6 13. f4 Nb4! 14. Rd1 Nxd3 15. Rxd3 Qb6 16. Be3 Bc5! { Well OK, this is our good bishop and it does break up our bishop-pair, yet
the move is good for the following reasons:
1. It allows Black not to fall too far behind in development.
2. Black’s remaining light-squared bishop may be activated with a vengeance
if Black achieves ... d5-d4 and ... Bc6.
3. It follows the principle: The defending and material-up side should seek
swaps to dilute the opponent’s attack. } *


