1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bd7 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Qb6 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nbd2!? { White reasons that a knight posted on c3 will be shut out of play with
Black’s ... a7-a6. So White instead posts it on d2, where it has access to b3, so that it fights for control over the critical d4- and c5-squares.
keep the double mindset when playing Black against Milner-Barry:
1. The line is only semi-sound for White.
2. White gets excellent practical chances to confuse us! } 9... Nxf3+ { Principle: The material up side should swap pieces } 10. Nxf3 h6 { eliminates White’s Ng5 tricks. } 11. Be3 Bc5 { We lose some time. In exchange we achieve another swap. } 12. Bxc5 Qxc5 13. Rc1 Qb6 14. b4?! { Theory ends here. White’s last move feels like a waste of time. I don’t
believe in White’s full compensation for the pawn since Black’s game remains solid. } *


