1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Be7 { I’m not a big fan of 5 ... Bb4 since:
1. I don’t really want to take the c3-knight, and give my opponent the
bishop-pair while strengthening White’s centre.
2. I feel that Black’s bishop is needed on e7 to defend against White’s
possible Bg5 pin. } 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 O-O 8. Nge2 Nbd7 9. O-O Nb6 10. Bb3 c6 11. Re1 Bf5 { A provocation. 11 ... Re8 would be more normal. } 12. Ng3 Bg6 13. f4 Bd6 14. Qf3 Qd7 15. Rf1 { This violates the Principles: Don’t retreat or move twice a piece in an open
position. } 15... Bf5 { Sometimes principles must deliberately be violated. Here the violation is:
Don’t hand your opponent the bishop-pair in an open position. My justification
of my move was that swaps help the defending side and the side playing against the isolani. } *


