1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Bd6 6. Nf3 Nge7 { Our sacred rule against the Exchange line is: Break the symmetry – even
slightly – whenever possible. In this way we frustrate many of our opponents’
attempts to drain the game of life and hold a draw. Since White moves his knight to f3, Balogh develops his to e7 } 7. O-O Bg4 8. Re1 Qd7 9. Nbd2 O-O-O 10. Qc2 (10. b4 Ng6 { [%cal Gg6f4,Gc6e7] } 11. b5 Nce7 12. a4 (12. Qa4 Kb8 13. Ne5 Bxe5 14. dxe5 d4! 15. c4 Nf4 16. Bf1 { Black has achieved an unbalanced and dynamically even
game which is certainly no longer drawish }) 12... Nf4 13. Bf1 Neg6 14. Qb3 Rhe8 15. Re3 Qf5 { I prefer Black’s attacking chances to White’s since so many of his pieces have gathered in the vicinity of White’s king, }) 10... f6 { In preparation of a pawn storm with ... g7-g5. } 11. b4 g5 12. b5 Na5! { The knight helps fight for the c4-square, since White at some point will
probably need to play c3-c4 } 13. Qa4 b6 14. Ba3 Kb8 15. Bxd6 Qxd6 16. c4!? dxc4 17. Nxc4 Nxc4 18. Qxc4 Bc8!? { It’s not cowardice if you decide to take refuge from a future storm. He feels
better with the bishop by his king’s side. Perhaps Black should risk 18 ... Bxf3! } 19. Rac1 Nd5 20. Nd2 Rhe8 21. Rxe8 Rxe8 22. Bxh7? { Principle: Don’t grab pawns in front of your king. } 22... Rh8 (22... Nf4 { Even stronger } 23. Bd3 Bb7 24. g3 Nxd3 25. Qxd3 Qd5 26. f3 Qxa2) *


