1. e4 e6 2. c4 { With this move White veers from the officialdom of main line theory. The
move can simply lead to the c2-c4 line of the Exchange French, or as in this
case, if White double captures then we get a slightly different version where
White takes on an isolani. } 2... d5 3. cxd5 (3. exd5 exd5 4. d4 { 3 exd5 exd5 4 d4 simply transposes to the c2-c4 lines of the Exchange
French, which we already looked at. }) 3... exd5 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Bb5+ (5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Bc4 Nb6! { I would avoid 6 ... Be6 7 Qb3 } 7. Bb3 Nc6 8. Nge2 Bd6 9. d4 O-O 10. O-O Bf5 $15) 5... Nbd7 { I’m in no rush to regain my pawn. White can’t hang on to it forever. } 6. Nc3 Be7 7. d4 O-O 8. Nge2 Nb6 9. Qb3?! Nfxd5 10. O-O Be6 $15 *


