1. e4 e6 2. f4 { This odd move is nothing to worry about and is usually the product of White
merely attempting to dodge French theory } 2... d5 3. e5 (3. exd5 { is an Exchange French transposition we actually don’t mind, since
f2-f4 is clearly an inferior substitute to the normal d2-d4. }) (3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 $10) 3... c5 { We toss this in before White solidifies his centre with d2-d4. } 4. Nf3 (4. c3 Nc6 5. d4?! { is an inferior Advanced Variation for White, who is in essence down a full move due to his f2-f4 move } 5... Nh6 6. Nf3 Qb6 7. Bd3 (7. Na3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bxa3 9. bxa3 Nf5) 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Bd7 9. Bc2 Nb4) 4... Nc6 5. c3 Nh6! { This move will usually scare White away from his intended d2-d4. } 6. Na3 (6. d4?! Qb6 { transposes to the above note, which is favourable for Black. }) 6... Bd7 7. Nc2 Qb6 8. d4 cxd4 9. cxd4 Nf5 10. g4 { almost forced } (10. Be2?! h5 { favours Black, who ties White down to coverage of d4 }) 10... Nfe7 11. Bd3 h5! { closing the kingside favours Black, who normally operates on the
queenside in Advanced French positions } 12. g5 g6 13. Bd2 Nf5 14. Bc3 Be7 15. a4 Na5 16. Bxa5 Qxa5+ $10 *


