1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. e3 { ‘wall’ of pawns stretching from f5-e6-d5-c6-b7 is most effective when it blocks the enemy bishop on g2. if white avoids bishop development on g2 than black will play in the spirit of the Nimzo- and Queen’s Indian Defences, having already placed a pawn on f5, which is generally a positive feature of his position. } 3... Nf6 4. Bd3 b6 5. Ne2 Bb7 6. f3 { he idea is to restrict the b7-bishop and support an
eventual e3-e4, building a strong centre. } 6... g6 { Since White’s plan involves e3-e4, our bishop will be ideally placed on g7, and a well-timed ...c5 will provide counterplay. } 7. Nbc3 Bg7 8. O-O O-O 9. e4 fxe4 10. fxe4 c5 { This is the only way to obtain suitable counterplay } 11. d5 { White gains space and tries to play against the lightsquared bishop on b7, but in return Black gains the important e5-outpost, and counterplay on the dark squares in general. } (11. e5 { leaves White overextended in the centre } 11... Ng4 12. Rxf8+ Qxf8 13. Nf4 Nh6 $17) (11. Bg5 { sets up a pin but it is not dangerous after: } 11... cxd4 12. Nxd4 Qc8) 11... Ng4 { Black launches his counterplay on the dark squares } 12. Rxf8+ Qxf8 13. Qe1 Na6 *


