1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. Nf3 { This section deals with lines involving an early Nf3 and e2-e3, where White avoids an early Nc3 – or at least delays it for long enough to avoid a simple transposition to one of the 3.Nc3 lines, as covered later under variation C. } 3... Nf6 4. e3 b6 { 4...d5 is a playable version of the Stonewall, when 5.Bd3 Bd6 6.0-0 c6 7.b3 Qe7 is the main line. However, I am not recommending it for a couple of reasons. Firstly, as mentioned earlier, my personal feeling is that the Stonewall it most effective against a bishop on g2. Secondly, we have seen in the previous variation that we are meeting 3.e3 Nf6 4.Bd3 with 4...b6, after which 5.Nf3 leads straight to the present game – so we may as well just focus on the ...b6 set-up here as well. } 5. Bd3 Be7 6. O-O O-O 7. b3 (7. Nc3 { is the other obvious idea, especially as there is no longer a transposition to any of the 3.Nc3 variants which will be analysed later } 7... Bb7 8. a3 { is a standard idea to gain space on the queenside, but Black is absolutely fine after: } 8... Ne4 { Before White has time for b2-b4. } 9. Ne2 c5 10. b3 Bf6 11. Bb2 d6 12. Qc2 Nd7 $10 { looks pleasant enough for Black. }) 7... Bb7 8. Bb2 (8. Nc3 Ne4 9. Bb2 c5 { Black must try to ruin White’s centre! He intends to follow up with ...Bf6, with the threat of ...Nxc3, ...Bxf3 and ...Nc6, with massive pressure on the d4-pawn. Therefore a logical try is: } { [%cal Ge7f6] } 10. d5 Nxc3 11. Bxc3 exd5 12. cxd5 Bf6 { This is Black’s safest option. } (12... Bxd5 { leads to complications : } 13. Ne5 d6 14. Bc4 Bxc4 15. Nxc4 d5 16. Qf3 $44)) 8... Ne4 9. Nfd2 { White is preparing f2-f3. } (9. Nc3 { transposes to the 8.Nc3 line in the previous note. }) (9. Nbd2 { offers Black a good game after the typical plan of : } 9... Bf6 10. Qc2 Nxd2 11. Nxd2 c5 { followed by ...Nc6. } { [%cal Gb8c6] }) 9... Na6 { Grischuk find an interesting way to finish development, which is faster than ...d6 followed by ...Nd7. The knight is not bad at all on a6. } 10. f3 Nd6 { Grischuk shows great understanding! White’s knight is not well placed on d2 and does not have a great future in general, so why trade knights? } 11. Nc3 Bf6 12. Ne2 c5 *


