1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 b6 (4... Bb4 { It is worth showing the problems with this: I once played this move instinctively and got into trouble. Later in variation C3, we will see the bishop coming here with good effect, but only after White has committed his knight to f3. Here White can simply play: } 5. Ne2! { This offers him a comfortable position with every chance of an opening advantage. } 5... b6 6. a3 { Black faces an unpleasant choice: he can give up the bishop pair “for nothing” or waste time retreating his bishop to a less-than-ideal place. } 6... Bxc3+ 7. Nxc3 Bb7 8. d5 $14) 5. Bd3 (5. Nf3 Bb4 { transposes to 5.e3 b6 in the notes to variation C3 }) 5... Bb7 6. f3!? { This is White’s sharpest plan in Nimzo- and Queen’s Indian structures. This move has many ideas. First of all is to restrict the light-squared bishop on b7, to take control of the e4-square and to build up the centre with e3-e4. } 6... c5! { Black should confront his opponent in the centre, otherwise e3-e4 will give White a huge space advantage. } 7. Nge2 (7. d5 exd5 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Nxd5 Bxd5 10. Bxf5 Bb7!) 7... Nc6 8. O-O Bd6 { This is clearly the most active place for the bishop; it keeps an eye on the h2-pawn and also prevents Nf4. } 9. d5 { The most consistent and ambitious approach. With this typical advance, White is forcing events. } (9. Bd2 O-O 10. a3 Rc8 11. Rb1 Nh5 $132 { [%cal Gc5d4,Gd8h4] }) 9... Ne5 10. e4 { White has established a seemingly strong pawn chain, but Black successfully undermines it. } 10... O-O 11. exf5 { White declares his hand in the centre; it is hard to suggest anything better. } (11. Nb5 { achieves nothing after } 11... Bb8 { followed by ...a6. } { [%cal Ga7a6] }) 11... exd5 12. cxd5 Nxd3 13. Qxd3 Qc7 14. h3 { This natural-looking move is rather risky. } (14. Kh1 { is safer, indirectly defending the h2-pawn while moving the king away from the dangerous
diagonal. Still, Black should be happy after } 14... c4 (14... Bxh2? 15. f4) 15. Qd4 Qc5 16. Qxc5 Bxc5 17. Nf4 Rae8 $44 { with excellent compensation for a pawn; White’s pawn on d5 is doomed. }) 14... c4 15. Qd1 Bc5+ { Black is in a hurry to recapture a pawn! } 16. Kh1 Qe5 *


