1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. f3 { White intends to seize the center with e2-e4. } 4... Bb4!? { ავტორი არ ამბობს მაგრამ მგონი საჭიროა e4 ის გასაჩერებლად } (4... d5 { A possible reason for avoiding 4...d5 can be found after: } 5. Bg5 Be7 6. e3 O-O 7. Bd3 c5 (7... c6 { is rather passive and } 8. Nge2 { leaves Black with some difficulties; for instance } 8... Nbd7? { is no good because of } 9. Nf4) 8. Nge2 Nc6 9. O-O { White has the more comfortable position, and in the long term Black will have problems with the weakness on e6. }) 5. Bd2 O-O 6. a3 Bxc3 7. Bxc3 d6 { With this logical move, Black prepares to gain space in the center with ...e5. } (7... b5!? { is an intriguing new idea } 8. cxb5 Nd5 9. Qd2 a6 10. e3 axb5 11. Bxb5 Qh4+ 12. g3 (12. Qf2? Nxe3) 12... Qh5 $44 { White faces some difficulties in finishing the development of his kingside.
Black can combine play along the open queenside files with pressure along the a8-h1 diagonal and against the weak squares f3 and e3 in White’s camp. }) 8. e3 Qe8!? { This was a new idea at the time, which has yet to be repeated. The main idea is to prepare ...e5, just like the more popular 8...Qe7 (as well as 8...Nc6). By choosing the e8-square for his queen, Black maintains some extra possibilities for transferring his queen to an active post on either flank. } 9. Qd2 Nc6 10. Bd3 e5 11. Ne2 (11. d5 Ne7 { White’s center will be undermined after ...b5. } { [%cal Gb7b5] }) 11... e4 12. Bc2 b5 $132 { An excellent, thematic sacrifice with the idea to gain control over the d5-square for the knights. Black’s light-square strategy also fits in well with the earlier 8...Qe8. } 13. cxb5 Ne7 14. fxe4 (14. a4?! { White did not have time to worry about the not-so-important b5-pawn. After 14.a4?! his king becomes unsafe and Black can play } 14... exf3 15. gxf3 Ned5 $40 { with great prospects }) 14... Nxe4! *


