1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 { This is the most popular move. } 4... Bb4 { As mentioned before, Black’s idea is to play an improved version of a Nimzo-Indian. This move is well timed, as White does not have the option of bringing the other knight to e2. } (4... d5 { The present position is just about the worst possible time for Black to commit to a Stonewall set-up: } 5. Bf4 c6 6. e3 Be7 (6... Bd6 { would be a good move if White was compelled to exchange on d6, but the problem is that he can simply maintain the tension } 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O Bxf4 9. exf4 { he ensuing change in the pawn structure helps White, but it was hard for Black to maintain the tension because both of his queenside minor pieces want to use the d7-square. White’s advantage is obvious : he has a
secure outpost on e5 for the knight, his pawn structure is favourable and Black has no counterplay. }) 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Qc2 Ne4 9. g4! { Here is the real problem: White does not just want to play a positional game center on the e5- square, but instead gets ready to open the g-file while undermining Black’s center }) 5. g3 (5. Bd2 { This is a natural way for White to avoid any damage to his pawn structure. } 5... O-O 6. g3 b6 7. Bg2 Bb7 { transposing to the 7.Bd2 0-0 line }) (5. Qb3 a5 { I find this the most logical continuation } 6. g3 (6. a3 a4! 7. Qc2 (7. Qxb4? Nc6 8. Qb5 Ra5) 7... Bxc3+ 8. Qxc3 O-O 9. g3 b6 10. Bg2 Bb7 $10 { White is stuck for ideas on the queenside and the b3-square is weak }) 6... b6 7. Bg2 Bb7 8. O-O O-O 9. Bg5 { White intends to eliminate the knight, as it controls important squares in the center such as e4 and d5. } (9. d5 { is thematic, but } 9... Na6! { followed by ...Nc5 gives Black nice play } { [%cal Ga6c5] }) 9... Bxc3 10. Qxc3 h6 11. Bxf6 Qxf6) (5. Qc2 { is playable but it seems a little slow. Black plays } 5... O-O 6. a3 Bxc3+ 7. Qxc3 b6 { reaching a typical set-up with good chances. }) (5. Bg5 { is nothing to fear. Black continues with natural moves such as } 5... O-O 6. e3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 d6 8. Be2 Nbd7 9. O-O b6 $10 { [%cal Gc8b7,Gd8e8] }) (5. e3 { also proves to be harmless after } 5... b6 6. Bd3 Bb7 7. O-O Bxc3 8. bxc3 O-O { when Black has a pleasant version of a Nimzo-Indian. He has a few possible plans, but I would tend to favour a set-up with ...c5 and ...d6, keeping the position closed and playing against White’s bishop pair and damaged pawn structure. }) 5... b6 6. Bg2 Bb7 7. O-O Bxc3 8. bxc3 O-O 9. d5!? { This is always a double-edged decision. White weakens his pawn structure some more, but on the other hand he is trying to open the position for his bishop pair while playing against the bishop on b7. } 9... Ne4! (9... exd5 { The greedy 9...exd5?! plays into White’s hands, and } 10. Nh4 { offers him a better position }) 10. Ba3 { More in the spirit of the position } (10. dxe6 { White forces an endgame but he seems to have misevaluated it. } 10... dxe6 11. Qxd8 Rxd8 12. Ng5 { This was clearly White’s idea. } 12... Re8 13. Nxe4 Bxe4 14. Bxe4 fxe4 $15 { It is clear that the black knight has better prospects than the enemy bishop. The target in White’s position is the c4-square. }) 10... Re8 { Only one game exists in the database, in which Black reacted badly with 10...d6?, and after got into huge trouble } 11. Nd4 c5! { This move needs to be remembered! } 12. dxc6 Nxc6 13. Bxe4 fxe4 14. Nb5 Na5! 15. Nd6 Qc7 { With a complex game } 16. Nxe8 Rxe8 *


