{ This chapter has dealt with several options after 1.d4 e6 2.c4 f5 where White avoids an early g2-g3. We started with the tricky 3.e3!? followed by 4.Bd3, when White keeps his knights flexible and can later look to expand in the centre with f2-f3 and e3-e4. I found the double fianchetto to be a good
choice for Black, leading to plenty of dynamic counterplay.

Next we considered 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.e3, when White omits Nc3, or at least delays it for long enough to discourage Black’s bishop from going to b4. Instead we play 4...b6 followed by ...Bb7 and ...Be7. Game 1 contained a lot of instructive moments, especially Grischuk’s ...Ne4-d6 manoeuvre, followed
by ...c5 and ...e5, which gave Black excellent play in the centre and on the kingside.

3.Nc3 Nf6 is the main line of the chapter. Then 4.e3 should be met by 4...b6 again, and after 5.Bd3 Bb7 6.f3 Black gets a good position with 6...c5!, challenging the centre before White has time for e3-
e4, as shown in Game 2. Next we looked at the aggressive 4.f3!?, when 4...Bb4 is a good way to prevent e2-e4. In Game 3 we saw White putting his trust in the bishop pair, while Black countered with a lead in development and play on the light squares. The ...b5 pawn sacrifice is an important
theme, which should offer Black plenty of counterplay.

Finally 4.Nf3 is the most popular continuation, when 4...Bb4 is a good reply, bearing in mind that White no longer has the convenient plan of putting the other knight on e2 and playing a2-a3. Instead his main options are 5.g3, 5.Bd2 and 5.Qb3, as analysed in Games 4, 5 and 6 respectively. In most cases Black will opt for a queenside fianchetto, followed by exchanging on c3 at some point, leading to positions resembling the Nimzo- and Queen’s Indian Defences. The difference is that Black already has his pawn on f5 rather than f7, which tends to help him slightly, as it improves his control
over the crucial e4-square while also making a Bg5 pin far less troublesome. }
 *


