1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 d5 5. Bf4 { White wants to discourage ...Bd6. } 5... c6 6. Nf3 Be7 { Normally it would be a concession to develop the bishop here rather than on d6 Introduction. However, White’s bishop has come to f4 prematurely and it will prove to be slightly exposed. } 7. Nbd2 Ne4 8. O-O Nd7 9. Ne5 O-O 10. Rc1 Nxe5 11. Bxe5 Nd6! { This manoeuvre should be remembered. The idea is to transfer the knight to f7, covering the e5- square. } 12. Bxd6 { It is hard to recommend anything better. The bishop was not doing much on e5, and White should exchange it now before it becomes a problem. } 12... Bxd6 13. e3 Bd7 14. Qb3 Qb6 15. Qc2 Qd8 16. Qb3 Qb8 { I tried to avoid a quick draw, but without much success. } 17. Nf3 b5! { This was my idea, and it’s a decent one, but it does not change the fact that the position is equal } 18. c5 (18. cxd5 cxd5 19. Ne1 Qb6 20. Nd3 Rfc8 $10) 18... Bc7 19. Ne1 g5 20. f4 h6 $10 { Here I decided to offer a draw, which my opponent accepted. The position is blocked, so the bishop
pair is not particularly useful, and I did not see how I could break through White’s firm position. } *


