1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 d5 5. Nf3 c6 6. O-O Bd6 7. Bf4 { This move used to be regarded as a big problem for Black, and it was the reason why Mikhail Botvinnik preferred to develop the bishop to e7. The prevailing opinion at the time was simply that Black should avoid exchanging his “good” bishop. Towards the end of the 1990s, 5...Bd6 became
more popular, as it became clear that the positions after the trade of bishops on f4 were not as one sided as was previously thought. Credit must go to GMs Artur Yusupov, Evgeny Gleizerov and Mikhail Ulybin, who demonstrated ways to create meaningful counterplay on the kingside, endangering the white king. } 7... Bxf4 8. gxf4 O-O { White’s big achievement is that he has traded the dark-squared bishops, but he has paid a price in the doubling of his pawns. White will try to exploit his space advantage and control over the e5-square, whereas Black needs to play actively on the kingside.
White will try to exploit his space advantage and
control over the e5-square, whereas Black needs to play actively on the kingside. The typical ...Ne4 will almost always feature in his plans. Once that move has been played, Black will look to activate his rook, usually with ...Rf6-h6(or-g6), but sometimes with ...Kh8, ...Rg8 and ...g5. Black also needs to think about how to activate his remaining bishop. He may accomplish this with the typical manoeuvre ...Bd7-e8-h5, or in some rare cases with ...b6 and ...Ba6. On the other hand, sometimes Black will just want to get on with his attack and leave the bishop
We will analyse B11) 9.Nc3 and B12) 9.e3, although I must stress that transpositions are possible (in particular, White almost always plays e2-e3 sooner or later) and understanding the middlegame plans tends to be more important that learning variations. } *


