1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 d5 5. Nf3 c6 6. O-O Bd6 7. Bf4 Bxf4 8. gxf4 O-O 9. Nc3 Nbd7 10. e3 Qe7!? { This flexible move is my favourite. Black strengthens his position and waits for White to play Ne5 before placing his own knight on e4. Once that has taken place, Black can decide whether to attack by transferring his rook via f6, or by preparing ...g5. } (10... Ne4 { This natural-looking move has been the most popular choice, but White has an effective way of meeting it. } 11. Nxe4 fxe4 12. Nd2! (12. Ne5 { The more popular 12.Ne5?! is a positional mistake in view of } 12... Nxe5 13. fxe5 Bd7 $15 { when Black’s bishop has better prospects than its counterpart; the plan is ...Qg5 (or ...Qh4) followed
by ...Be8-h5. }) (12. Ng5 { is slightly inaccurate due to } 12... Nf6 13. f3 h6 14. Nh3 exf3 $10 { White’s knight is poorly placed on h3. }) 12... Nf6 13. f3! exf3 14. Nxf3 Qe7 15. Ne5 Bd7 $146 (15... Nd7 16. Qb3! Nxe5 17. fxe5 $14 { lead in development }) 16. Rf3! { This manoeuvre creates some difficulties for Black } 16... Be8 17. Qc2! Nd7 18. Rh3 h6 $14 { Black is on the defensive in a pretty one-sided position. }) 11. Ne5 Ne4! (11... Nxe5 { I did not want to hurry with this } 12. fxe5 Ne4 { Black has actually scored 3/3 from here in practical games, but I was concerned about } 13. Ne2 { followed by f2-f3, when I did not see a good route for my knight. } { [%cal Gf2f3] }) 12. Ne2 { White should avoid trading knights, as Black’s “bad” bishop will become more active than White’s supposedly “good” one. } (12. f3?! Nxc3 13. bxc3 Nxe5 14. fxe5 (14. dxe5 dxc4 $15) 14... f4 $15) 12... Rf6! 13. f3 Nd6 14. c5 (14. b3 Rh6 15. Qe1? { White reacts with panic to the prospect of ...Qh4, which was not yet a real threat. 15.Rc1 was preferable, and if 15...Qh4 White defends with 16.h3= followed by Qe1. } 15... dxc4! { Black exploits the slight shakiness of the c4-point. Breaking up his own pawn structure is justified by the fast activation of the light-squared bishop. } 16. Nxc4 Nxc4 17. bxc4 b6 { White’s problems with the c4-pawn and inactive bishop are obvious. } { [%cal Gc8a6,Gc6c5,Ga8c8] }) 14... Nf7 15. Rc1 (15. b4 Rh6 16. Qe1 Nfxe5 17. fxe5 b6! { [%cal Gc8a6] }) 15... Rh6 16. Qe1 Ndxe5 17. fxe5 Bd7 18. f4 Nh8! 19. b4 Ng6 $132 { Black intends to organize his pieces with ...Nh4, ...Kh8 and ...Rg8 in preparation for launching his attack with ...g5, and he may even include ...Be8-h5 somewhere along the way as well. } { [%cal Gg6h4,Gg8h8,Ga8g8,Gg7g5,Gd7e8,Ge8h5] } *


