1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 d5 5. Nf3 Bd6 6. O-O c6 7. Bf4 Bxf4 8. gxf4 O-O 9. e3 Nbd7 { 9...Bd7?! has been tried by some strong players but is it really worth spending three tempos to bring the bishop to the ot-so-fantastic h5-square? } 10. Nbd2 (10. Ne5 { White’s idea is to play a quick f2-f3, but it has some shortcomings. } 10... Ne4 11. f3 Nd6 12. c5?! { This move looks tempting, but it actually wastes time and enables Black to gain some initiative } (12. cxd5!? exd5 13. Qe1 $10) 12... Nxe5 13. fxe5 Nc4! { 13...Nf7 has been the choice of a few strong players, and although Black’s position is quite acceptable after that move, I must prefer the dynamic approach featured in the game. } 14. Qc1 f4 { A typical blow! This temporary pawn sacrifice ruins White’s pawn structure and stops him from playing f3-f4. } 15. exf4 b6! { An excellent move, opening a second front and undermining White’s centre. } 16. f5 { This kind of panic reaction can often occur when under pressure. } (16. b3 Na5 17. Nc3 Qh4 18. cxb6 axb6 19. Na4 Nc4 20. Nc3 Na5 21. Na4 $10) 16... Rxf5 17. b3 Qh4! 18. f4 Rh5! 19. h3 (19. bxc4? Qxh2+ 20. Kf2 Rh4 21. Qe3 Ba6 22. Nd2 Rg4 23. Rg1 bxc5 $19) 19... Na5 $36) 10... Qe7 { Please keep in mind that 10...Ne4 should be avoided, as 11.Nxe4 transposes to the note to Black’s 10th move in the previous variation, where it was shown that 11...fxe4 12.Nd2! favours White. } 11. Qc2 (11. Ne5 { enables Black to equalize relatively easily with } 11... Nxe5 12. dxe5 (12. fxe5 Ne4 13. f4 b6 $10) 12... Ne4 13. Rc1 b6 $10) (11. Rc1 { Another way for White to improve his position before landing the knight on e5 } 11... Ne4 { Compared with the 10...Ne4 line that we rejected, the inclusion of the moves ...Qe7 and Rc1 helps Black. } 12. Nxe4 fxe4 13. Nd2 Nf6 14. f3 exf3 15. Nxf3 Bd7 16. Ne5 Be8 17. Qb3 Bh5 { Unlike the analogous line given on page 62, White did not have time for the Rf3-h3 manoeuvre here, so Black has an easier time }) 11... Ne4 { Once again, Black obtains an improved version of the 10...Ne4 line that we rejected, but this time a radical change of approach will be needed. } 12. Nxe4 { This is definitely the critical move. } (12. Ne5?! Nxe5 13. fxe5 { is rather feeble, and Black has more than one way to get a good game. The
simplest is: } 13... Nxd2!? 14. Qxd2 f4 15. exf4 Qh4 $15 { White has some problems due to his kingside weaknesses. }) 12... dxe4! $146 { Recapturing with the d-pawn is a rare occurrence in the Bf4 variation, but here it is a necessary improvement. The main idea is to play ...c5, with counterplay in the centre and on the queenside. } (12... fxe4 { will soon transpose to an earlier note which we already rejected as favouring White. } 13. Nd2 Nf6 14. f3 exf3 15. Nxf3 Bd7 16. Ne5 Be8 17. Rf3!) 13. Ne5 c5! 14. f3 cxd4 15. exd4 e3 { The pawn obviously will not survive for long, but it is more important to keep White’s bishop closed off } 16. Qe2 Nxe5 17. dxe5 Qc5 18. Rfe1 b6 19. b3 Bb7 20. Qxe3 Qxe3+ 21. Rxe3 Rad8 $44 { Black maintains full compensation for the sacrificed pawn. } *


